With the second beta of <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/ios-27/">iOS 27</a>, Apple added support for replying to a specific message in an <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/rcs/">RCS</a> conversation with an Android user.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2025/03/RCS-Feature-1.jpg" alt="" width="2500" height="1406" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-994897" /><br/>
You can now long press on a message to get to the option to reply, and it works the same way that it does with iMessage.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/06/ios-27-rcs-replies.jpg" alt="" width="2000" height="1125" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1053321" /><br/>
&zwnj;iOS 27&zwnj; also displays tapback/reaction emoji to images and videos properly. In <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/ios-26/">iOS 26</a>, it would use a text descriptor, such as [x loved an image] instead of showing the emoji. In &zwnj;iOS 27&zwnj;, the emoji shows up on the image or video as it does in an iMessage conversation.<br/>
<br/>
Apple added support for &zwnj;RCS&zwnj; in iOS 18, and has been improving it since then. iOS 26.5 added encryption for &zwnj;RCS&zwnj; messages sent between iPhone and Android users, and now more of the &zwnj;RCS&zwnj; features are being implemented.<br/>
<br/>
For &zwnj;RCS&zwnj; reply threading to work properly, both the sender and the recipient need to have a smartphone and carrier that supports &zwnj;RCS&zwnj;.<br/>
<br/>
